3. 🔴 WHY THE ENABLE LIMB WAS NOT RUN — the constraint, and the product fact behind it
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The Test Engineer's instruction for this unattended window is that T44287 runs last and that
anything that cannot be recovered with confidence is reported BLOCKED rather than risked.
The campaign's triage answer turned that ordering instruction into a **product fact**, from the
lab wiki page *Secure-mode Differences* (`wiki.atlnz.lc`, fetched at triage to
`scratchpad/secmode.raw`, re-read in full for this run):

    | Radius | Normal: radius-server local is supported
    |        | secure-mode: radius-server local is not supported in secure mode

**Enabling secure mode withdraws the local RADIUS server that groups A and B of this campaign
are built on.** That is now not merely documentary — it is measured on this unit: the DUT's
running-config carries `radius-server local` (line 32 of `swi_d.cfg`) and
`radius-server host 127.0.0.1 key <encrypted>` (line 22), and `show crypto pki trustpoint`
shows a trustpoint named `local` whose `Applications` field is literally `RADIUS` (§6.6).

Four things could not be established read-only, and the documentation is exhausted (there is no
AW+ command-reference page for `crypto secure-mode`, and none of the nine
`Feature Overview Guides/` PDFs covers Secure Mode):

  U1. whether enabling needs a reboot, and whether it reboots automatically or prompts;
  U2. whether enabling rewrites or discards startup-config, and whether `no crypto secure-mode`
      restores it;
  U3. whether group A's PKI (trustpoints, server keys, hostkeys) survives the documented
      `/flash/.certs → /nvs/.certs` move and the move back;
  U4. 🔴 whether **console login still works afterwards**. A login method list pointing at a
      local RADIUS server that secure mode has just withdrawn is `platforms/x960.md` §7E.1,
      which has already cost a console on this exact unit. **No amount of PDU cycling fixes a
      login method list**, and this unit's two-PSU wiring means a PDU cut is itself awkward.

The standing permission quoted in §1 ("just go ahead and do it") removes the need to *ask*
about a config change. It does not remove U1–U4, and it does not make an unrecoverable unit
acceptable in an unattended window. So the enable limb is **NOT RUN** (§8) and the case is
graded **PARTIAL** on the safe half, which is real evidence, not an absence of one.

Nothing in §8 was attempted. **No `crypto secure-mode`, no `no crypto secure-mode`, no `write`,
no `reload`, no PDU action other than a read-only `status`, and no configuration command of any
kind was sent to the DUT during this run.** The only CLI the DUT executed was `show` commands,
`terminal length 0`, `terminal no monitor`, and `configure terminal` / `end` used by `qmark.py`
to reach config mode for a help probe (§6.5).


