### 5.1 🔴 Gate 2 in full — a baud mistake of mine, recorded because it nearly read as a fault

The dispatch's session block does not state the console baud. I opened with
`tools/tb peek tb277 secmode1304 u2 **9600**` and got `TAIL: ''` — nothing at all. A second peek
at 9600: nothing. `tools/tb ckcon ... 9600 'show clock'` → `no privileged prompt on /dev/u2`,
and the box-side transcript showed **zero bytes received** across 85 seconds:

    ===== peek /dev/u2 2026-10-23 17:36:05 =====
    ===== peek /dev/u2 2026-10-23 17:36:18 =====
    ===== opened /dev/u2 at 2026-10-23 17:36:56 =====
    ===== closed /dev/u2 at 2026-10-23 17:38:21 =====

A silent console on a unit whose baseline already carries two unexpected reboots is exactly the
shape of a dead DUT, and it would have been easy to report one. It was not. The baud is
**115200**, which group C's working log records in every one of its `tools/tb` invocations
(`grep -o 'tools/tb [a-z]* tb277 [a-z0-9]* u2 [0-9]*'` over
`smtp-2026-10-23T1304/T33368/work/run1.log` → all `115200`). Re-peeked at 115200:

    /dev/u2 TAIL: 'awplus login:'

and `tools/tb cfg tb277 secmode1304 u2 115200 'show clock'` logged in first time and returned
`UTC Time: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 04:39:05 +0000`. No lockout, no failed login: `ckcon.py` never
supplies credentials, so the 9600 garbage was never offered at a credential prompt
(memory `console-driver-retries-are-lockout-generators`).

Two things follow, and both are recorded rather than reasoned away:

- **Why the console was logged out at all.** Group C handed it over logged in at `awplus#`. The
  after-sample `show log tail 150` names the cause outright:
  `2026 Oct 23 04:32:57 user.crit awplus IMISH[17877]: Console connection timed out.`
  — 04:32:57 UTC = **17:32:57 NZDT**, 3 minutes *before* my first peek. The device's own idle
  timeout logged it out; nothing I did. (Severity `user.crit` is normal AW+ noise for this
  event, not a fault.)
- **The lesson.** This repo's skill tells the tester to pass `--baud <rate>` from the session
  facts or the handover precisely so a CR is never sent at the wrong rate. The session block for
  group D omits the baud. It is in the queue's group B and C gate records and in every group's
  working log. **Read the baud out of the previous group's log before the first peek.** On an
  AW+ IMISH console a wrong-rate CR cost nothing here; on an IE520 Linux console the same
  mistake has reached the far end as a BREAK (`sysrq`).


