/flash/.nvs" caveat does not apply to it.

### 6.5 Method note — how the CLI surface was read without executing anything

Every help probe used `tools/tb qmark`, which types `PREFIX ?` with **no CR** and then clears
the line with Ctrl-U, and which self-tests that mechanism before each pass. `qmark.py` leaves
the console back at exec with `end`. `<cmd> ?` must never go through a CR driver, because a
trailing CR executes the command whenever `<cr>` is valid — and in this case `crypto
secure-mode` in config mode is precisely a command for which `<cr>` *is* valid. Had the surface
been read with `ckcon`, the probe itself would have enabled secure mode. (memory
`never-send-cli-help-through-a-cr-driver`.)

One cosmetic artefact, recorded so it is not mistaken for a device complaint: with no `--mode`
list, `tools/tb` passes the literal `--` through to `qmark.py` as a prefix, and the DUT's help
parser answered `-- ?` with `% Unrecognized command`. Nothing was executed; the Ctrl-U cleared
it. Drop the `--` when there is no `--mode`.

### 6.6 STEP 5 — the four unknowns, narrowed as far as read-only allows

Three of the four attended-window **preconditions** in the recovery plan could be *measured*
read-only, and all three are already satisfied. This is the part of the run with the most value
for whoever gets the attended window:

**(a) The console login does NOT depend on RADIUS — U4's worst case is already excluded.**
From `swi_d.cfg` (lines 25–27):

    aaa authentication enable default local
    aaa authentication login default local
    aaa authentication dot1x default group radius

and line 7:

    username manager privilege 15 password 8 $5$bJozIP4D$...

Login **and** enable both use the `local` method, with a known-good local account. Only the
**dot1x** method list points at `group radius`, and that group resolves to
`radius-server host 127.0.0.1` (line 22) — i.e. to `radius-server local` (line 32), the very
server secure mode withdraws. So the documented RADIUS withdrawal would break **802.1X
authentication on port1.0.48** (group A's rig), and would **not** break console or SSH login.
That retires the unrecoverable half of U4. It does not retire U4 entirely: it says the *known*
mechanism for losing the console is absent, not that no other exists.

**(b) Only privilege levels 1 and 15 are in use.** The only `username` line on the box is
`privilege 15`. Secure mode's "privilege levels 1 AND 15 only" restriction therefore has nothing
to invalidate here, and the companion rule — `enable password` must be at least 8 characters —
has no `enable password` line to apply to.

**(c) Both PSU outlets answer.** `tools/tb pdu tb277 10.36.230.212 1 status` → `outlet 1 on:
True`; `tools/tb pdu tb277 10.36.230.212 8 status` → `outlet 8 on: True`. Raw: `work/pdu-D.out`.
Read-only `status`, not `cycle`. The last-resort both-outlet power cut in §8 is therefore
available — which matters, because the run `.setup`'s `[power]` stanza can name only outlet 1,
so a framework-initiated cycle on this unit is a **silent no-op**: it "completes", both PSU bays
still read Ok, and only uptime shows nothing happened.

**(d) The PKI inventory that U3 puts at risk, recorded so an attended run can compare.**
`show crypto pki trustpoint` and `show crypto key hostkey` — raw: `work/pki-D.out`:

    Trustpoint "default-selfsigned"   Self-signed CA (RSA + ECDSA)
        Local Server (RSA)   : Enrolled with this trustpoint.
        Local Server (ECDSA) : Enrolled with this trustpoint.
        Server Key           : RSA: default-selfsigned / ECDSA: default-selfsigned_ecdsa
        Applications         : auth-web,cwm,http,stoat
    Trustpoint "default-system"       Non-authenticated trustpoint
    Trustpoint "local"                Self-signed CA (RSA + ECDSA)
        Local Server (RSA)   : Enrolled with this trustpoint.
        Local Server (ECDSA) : Enrolled with this trustpoint.
        Server Key           : RSA: server-default / ECDSA: server-default-ecdsa
        Applications         : RADIUS

    Type       Bits  Fingerprint
    rsa        3072  SHA256:iWG4XK1CWjkpR9bQM5MBAuYtiyt/PyBvY3yollNf54E
    ecdsa       384  SHA256:oer/uwNjbwLIMUamUN5UW92BuJA81Cyh6ScF/x6RSrc
    ed25519     256  SHA256:VhI6K5ajJHpjKXJ/vCsQSZPvEnlxxrOeMkrZRDXMgq4

Two things worth having:
- Trustpoint `local` is group A's, and its `Applications` field is literally `RADIUS` — the
  trustpoint and the server that secure mode withdraws are the same object. **That trustpoint
  already existed before this campaign and must not be removed** (queue, group A rig).
- The hostkeys are **rsa 3072, ecdsa 384, ed25519 256** — there is **no rsa1 and no dsa key on
  this unit**. The documented restriction "rsa1 and dsa keys are not supported in secure-mode"
  therefore has nothing to break here either. The three fingerprints above are the exact values
  an attended run should re-read after enabling, to settle U3.

**(e) The two remaining unknowns, U1 and U2, could not be narrowed at all.** Neither the CLI
help (`crypto secure-mode ?` → bare `<cr>`, no warning text) nor the wiki says whether enabling
reboots, whether it prompts, or what it does to startup-config. They stay open.

### 6.7 STEP 6 — the documented differences, quoted

From the lab wiki *Secure-mode Differences* (`wiki.atlnz.lc`, `action=raw`), the full
Normal-vs-secure-mode table. Fetched at triage to `scratchpad/secmode.raw` and re-read in full
for this run. Fifteen rows; each is a behaviour the NOT RUN limb would have to prove:

| feature | Normal | secure-mode |
| --- | --- | --- |
| encrypted passwords | `show run unencrypted` can be executed | `show run unencrypted` **CANNOT** be executed |
| NVS storage | NVS can be accessed in IMISH; non-NVS devices access `/flash/.nvs` | NVS **cannot** be accessed in IMISH; `/flash/.nvs` cannot be accessed on non-NVS devices |
| PKI, keys and certificates | stored in flash under `/flash/.certs` | stored in nvs under `/nvs/.certs` |
| Cryptographic algorithms for TLS | see *Cryptographic Algorithms* | see *Cryptographic Algorithms* |
| SSH | ciphers changeable with `secure-algs` and other `secure-*`; `ssh server disallow-cbc-ciphers` not allowed | ciphers **not** changeable except CBC via `ssh server disallow-cbc-ciphers`; `allow-legacy-ssh-rsa` not allowed; **rsa1 and dsa keys not supported** (`crypto key destroy userkey USERNAME (rsa\|dsa\|rsa1\|ecdsa\|ed25519)`) |
| `show post` | results from file system checks | results from post **and cryptographic algorithms/modules**; same crypto info via `crypto secure-mode-selftest` in EXEC mode |
| SNMP | SNMPv2c **and** SNMPv3 allowed | **only SNMPv3**; MD5 and DES not allowed for authentication; NVS file system not returned from snmpget/snmpwalk |
| Remote Access | SSH can be enabled; **telnet can be enabled** | SSH can be enabled; **telnet cannot be enabled**; CR-84010: telnet **client** is disabled |
| User Privileges | privilege levels 1–15 | privilege levels **1 AND 15** only; `enable password` requires ≥ 8 characters |
| TACACS+ | TACACS+ groups allowed | TACACS+ groups **NOT** allowed |
| Crypto Providers | OpenSSL default, TPM | **FIPS default**, OpenSSL, TPM, Jitter |
| NTP | MD5, SHA1 and SHA1-encrypted supported | **MD5 not supported**; SHA1 and SHA1-encrypted supported |
| Licensing | license activation allowed | license activation **not** allowed |
| Login banner | no login banner by default | configures `security_banner` when first enabled |
| Radius | `radius-server local` is supported | 🔴 `radius-server local` is **not supported** in secure mode |

Mapped onto the case title — "Disable Telnet, SNMPv1/v2, all privilege levels except 1 and 15,
weak crypto (MD5, RSA1, DSA)" — every clause of the title is one of these rows. The case is
asking for the documented difference list to be confirmed on the DUT.

This DUT's **starting point** for each title clause, measured from `swi_d.cfg`:
- telnet: `no service telnet` — **already off**, so the telnet limb needs telnet turned *on*
  first, then secure mode enabled, to prove the refusal;
- SNMP: `snmp-server` is Enabled (`show snmp-server`: SNMP Server Enabled, IPv4+IPv6, SNMPv3
  Authentication Max Fail 5, Lockout 300 s, engine ID
  `0x80001f88808e3cab4244f4d83800000000`) with **no community string configured** anywhere in
  running-config — so the SNMPv1/v2c limb likewise needs a v2c community added first;
- privilege levels: only 15 in use (§6.6b);
- weak crypto: no rsa1 and no dsa key present (§6.6d); `no banner motd` is set, so the
  `security_banner` difference is cleanly observable from this baseline.

🔴 **A caveat on the TPS row, found in the wiki and recorded as an observation, not a verdict.**
The companion page *Products with crypto secure-mode support* lists, as of 28/11/2024, the
platform feature flags that carry `crypto-secure-mode`: `arctx2, IE220, IE340, IE360, IE560
(cli only), IEx60, SBx908NG, SBx90xGEN3, swmrvl_armv7, swmrvl_armv8, x220, x250, x320, x330,
x530, x550, x930, XS900`. **The x960 is not named.** The DUT's own build string is
`sin_x96056-20261019-27` and its core-dump filename (§7) identifies the platform as
`swmrvl_armv8` — which **is** on the list, so the x960-56 is plausibly covered by that entry
rather than by a missing one. This is not a finding; it is a note that the wiki's device list is
dated and should not be used to contradict either the TPS row (226 = Yes) or the CLI, which on
this unit plainly offers the command.


