9. AFTER THE RUN
----------------

**Teardown: none, because nothing was set up.** No configuration command was sent to the DUT at
any point in this run (§3).

1. **Console.** Left at exec, read from the **box-side transcript**
   (`tools/tb tail tb277 /tmp/secmode1304/console-u2.log`), not from a tool's stdout:

       awplus#
       >>> terminal no monitor
       terminal no monitor
       awplus#
       ===== closed /dev/u2 at 2026-10-23 17:46:44 =====

   `terminal no monitor` was sent **from exec**, not from config mode. Not in config mode, not
   at a shell. Left **logged in at `awplus#`**, which is how groups A, B and C each handed over;
   this unit's own idle timeout logs the console out after a few minutes anyway (§5.1), and
   `/wrap-dt` owns the final disposition.

2. **Probe, after.** Re-run with the identical facts:
   `tools/tb probe tb277 --consoles u2 --no-prompt --pdu 10.36.230.212 --outlet u2=1 --name
   u2=swi_d --read-only --baud 115200 --template <the run .setup>` → exit **2 NEEDS-CHECK**,
   with **two** `CHECK_CABLE` lines where the before-probe had one:

       [portlink] tb-swi_d eth3   CHECK_CABLE  bench = (absent)  template = eth3-port1.0.10
       [portlink] tb-swi_d eth4   CHECK_CABLE  bench = (absent)  template = eth4-port1.0.48

   🔴 **The eth3 line is a measurement artefact of this run's own quietness, and it is NOT
   repaired, only reported.** `CHECK_CABLE` means the NIC has link but its MAC was not learned
   on any switch port. The DUT side was read directly to settle it (`work/eth3-check-D.out`):

       awplus# show interface port1.0.10 status
       port1.0.10  -   connected   10   a-full  a-1000  1000BASE-T

       awplus# show arp
       10.38.22.33   000d.b955.770e   vlan10   port1.0.10   dynamic

   The port is **connected**, in vlan 10, auto-negotiated full/1000, and the box's underlay
   address 10.38.22.33 is still resolved to its MAC **on port1.0.10**. Nothing moved. What
   happened is that group C's SMTP traffic over eth3 stopped at 17:22, the before-probe at 17:40
   still found the MAC in the FDB, and by the after-probe at 17:46 it had aged out (`arp ageing
   timeout 300`). This group sent no traffic — it is an all-`show` case — and `--read-only`
   correctly suppresses the probe's own host-NIC pings, so nothing refreshed it. The eth4 line is
   the standing one: port1.0.48 is dot1x-controlled with no supplicant authenticated, so the
   box's MAC is not in the FDB there either, and it has read `CHECK_CABLE` for groups B, C and D
   alike.

3. **Boot baseline.** Re-read at 17:45:26 — `Current boot image : flash:/x960-tb277.rel (file
   exists)`, `Current boot config: flash:/default.cfg (file exists)`, `Autoboot status :
   disabled`. **Unchanged.**

4. **Running-config drift.** `show running-config` captured before step 1 and again after the
   last step; `diff` of the two is **empty** — byte-identical, 126 lines. Both saved:
   `work/rc-before-D.txt`, `work/rc-after-D.txt`.

5. **`bench-state.md`.** Regenerated by both probe runs. Copies kept in the session scratchpad
   (`bench-state-groupD-pre.md`, `bench-state-groupD-post.md`); the tracked file
   `bench-setup/tb277/bench-state.md` was restored with `git checkout --` and is **not
   committed**.

6. **Nothing started, so nothing to stop.** No tcpdump, no sender, no watcher, no background
   process. This group ran no framework script and no host traffic tool.

7. **Group A's rig is exactly as found.** `radius-server local`, its users, the `local`
   trustpoint, port1.0.48's dot1x configuration, `vlan 60` and the vlan10 L3 underlay were read
   and not touched. The byte-identical running-config diff is the proof.


